div class=3D"note-content"><div><strong class=3D"note-content-field disable=
-tex-rendering">Comment:</strong> <div class=3D"note-content-value markdown=
-rendered"><p>Dear Area Chair,</p>
<p>We respectfully ask the Area Chair to read our response to Reviewer 27Jx=
 (#1) closely and to judge it on its merits. The single Reject (1) is a cle=
ar outlier: the other four reviewers rated the paper 3, 3, 3, and 4, callin=
g the direction "sensible," the problem "important" and "promising" (D8hx),=
 and the work "a fair and interesting contribution" with "compelling" match=
ed-setting results (wGgF). A Reject is the most severe rating, and we would=
 expect it to rest on a correspondingly serious, well-substantiated flaw. Y=
et Reviewer 27Jx points to no defect beyond two concerns, presentation and =
the distinction from Vgent (including the role of the event graph), both of=
 which the other reviewers also raised and none of which they judged disqua=
lifying. The review's main emphasis is that the manuscript reads as an unfi=
nished draft, which is a fixable presentation matter, not a fundamental pro=
blem with the method or its results. We also note, respectfully, that this =
was the last score entered, after the other reviews had become visible. We =
address both concerns below.</p>
<p><strong>(1) Presentation.</strong> These draft-stage errors stem from th=
e inexperience of a student first author, and we take responsibility for th=
em. The other reviewers did not treat them as fatal; one even rated the pre=
sentation "good" (wGgF). We have since carried out a careful, line-by-line =
revision of the manuscript, and we have verified that these were purely pre=
sentation issues, not substantive ones, and that the rigor of our conclusio=
ns and every claim, result, and number in the paper are unaffected.</p>
<p><strong>(2) SGDAgent and Vgent are fundamentally different.</strong> Vge=
nt is a fixed, retrieve-then-read graph-RAG over frame- and segment-local r=
elations, a single-pass pipeline. SGDAgent is an adaptive agent. It builds =
a multi-level (frame, segment, and event) scene-graph index, treats that in=
dex as the agent's primary retrieval index, and runs a multi-step propose-v=
erify-answer loop, checking every graph proposal against raw frames before =
it answers. This agentic, multi-scale retrieval, rather than any single lay=
er, is what drives the +18.2-pp matched-setting gain on LVBench (from 54.8%=
 to 73.0%, under the same backbone, captions, and budget).</p>
<p><strong>(3) Judging Stage 3 in isolation is one-sided.</strong> The subm=
itted ablation isolates only the Stage-3 step, where adding it to Stage 1+2=
 moves accuracy from 64.6% to 64.4%, within run-to-run variance. The compar=
ison that matters is the full three-level index against the frame-only base=
, which raises accuracy from 59.2% to 64.4% (+5.2 pp) while also improving =
efficiency. Stage 3 itself cuts LLM and frame-inspection calls by 71.5% and=
 74.7% at unchanged accuracy, so the agent reaches the same answers with fa=
r less search, which is already a reasoning gain. The submitted event-graph=
 construction is also deliberately conservative, not an intrinsic ceiling. =
In a follow-up with a strengthened, caption-free graph construction, the sy=
stem reaches 68.5%, a 3.9-pp gain over the segment-only version and 13.7 pp=
 over the caption-only baseline. This confirms that Stage 3 contributes gen=
uine accuracy when it is built carefully, while still reducing reasoning ro=
unds and tool calls. The same structured index also lets the lighter o4-min=
i reach 73.0%, matching DVD's far stronger o3 (74.2%). We will report these=
 strengthened, fairly matched results in the camera-ready.</p>
<p>We sincerely thank the Area Chair for the time taken to consider this.</=
p>
