trengths:</strong> <div class=3D"note-content-value markdown-rendered"><p>T=
he high-level idea of restructuring the evidence space for long-form video =
QA =E2=80=94 replacing flat caption search with a hierarchical scene-graph =
index and a propose-verify-answer agent loop =E2=80=94 is reasonable and ad=
dresses a real limitation of caption-centric agentic methods.
The matched-setting ablations in Section 4.3 are a good practice: holding t=
he backbone, caption source, and budget fixed isolates the contribution of =
the structured index more convincingly than the cross-method comparison in =
Table 1.</p>
</div></div><div><strong class=3D"note-content-field disable-tex-rendering"=
>Weaknesses:</strong> <div class=3D"note-content-value markdown-rendered"><=
p>The manuscript contains a large number of draft-stage errors, and most of=
 the text appears to have been submitted without substantive human polishin=
g. Examples include a dangling "Figure ??" reference, repeated typos such a=
s "multi-granul," "Constrion," and "Stage," and generally unedited phrasing=
 throughout. In its current state the paper does not meet the presentation =
bar expected for review.
The core distinction from VAgent is not adequately established. VAgent also=
 uses a graph-structured representation for video retrieval, and the paper =
differentiates itself only by claiming VAgent is "limited to frame- or segm=
ent-level local relations without cross-scale unification." But the cross-s=
cale event layer (Stage 3) is exactly what is supposed to provide this adva=
ntage, and the ablation in Table 3 shows that adding Stage 3 does not impro=
ve accuracy at all (64.6% =E2=86=92 64.4%). If the event layer contributes =
no accuracy gain, the central conceptual difference from VAgent largely col=
lapses, and the paper is left differentiating itself mainly on an efficienc=
y argument that it does not frame as its core contribution.</p>
</div></div><div><strong class=3D"note-content-field disable-tex-rendering"=
>Review:</strong> <div class=3D"note-content-value markdown-rendered"><p>Th=
is paper proposes SGDAgent, a long-form video understanding agent built on =
a three-level (frame/segment/event) spatio-temporal scene graph index, quer=
ied through a propose-verify-answer loop. The direction is sensible and the=
 matched-setting evaluation methodology is appreciated.
However, the paper has two problems that I consider disqualifying in its cu=
rrent form. First, the manuscript is clearly a draft: it contains an unreso=
lved "Figure ??" reference, numerous typographical errors, and unpolished p=
rose throughout. This is not a matter of a few isolated mistakes; the writi=
ng as a whole has not been carefully revised, which makes the paper difficu=
lt to evaluate fairly and signals it was submitted prematurely.
Second, and more fundamentally, the paper's claimed novelty relative to VAg=
ent rests on cross-scale hierarchical unification, embodied by the event-le=
vel graph (Stage 3). Yet the authors' own ablation (Table 3) shows Stage 3 =
yields no accuracy improvement =E2=80=94 it functions only as an efficiency=
 layer that reduces LLM and frame-inspection calls. If the component that s=
upposedly distinguishes SGDAgent from VAgent does not improve answer qualit=
y, then the core conceptual contribution over VAgent is not substantiated. =
The efficiency benefit is real and worth reporting, but it is not framed as=
 the paper's main claim and is not, on its own, sufficient to establish the=
 contribution.
Because of the combination of an unpolished manuscript and an insufficientl=
y justified core novelty claim, I recommend rejection. I would encourage th=
e authors to thoroughly revise the writing and to either demonstrate an acc=
uracy benefit from the hierarchical event layer or reframe the paper's cont=
ribution honestly around efficiency.</p>
