><div><strong class=3D"note-content-field disable-tex-rendering">Strengths:=
</strong> <div class=3D"note-content-value markdown-rendered"><p>This paper=
 tackles an important problem in long-form video understanding: how to effi=
ciently locate sparse and temporally distributed evidence in hour-scale vid=
eos. The motivation is strong, since caption-only retrieval and flat embedd=
ing search often fail to capture entity-level relations and long-range temp=
oral dependencies. SGDAgent converts open-ended caption search into graph-g=
uided candidate verification by building a hierarchical spatio-temporal sce=
ne graph index. The frame-, segment-, and event-level graph design, unified=
 by a shared entity registry, is a reasonable way to represent multi-scale =
video semantics. The agent design is intuitive. The propose-verify-answer p=
aradigm uses graph retrieval to propose candidate regions and frame inspect=
ion to verify visual evidence. This avoids treating the scene graph as a di=
rect answer source and instead uses it to structure the search process. The=
 case study provides a clear illustration of how graph retrieval can progre=
ssively narrow the search from event to segment to frame-level evidence.</p=
>
</div></div><div><strong class=3D"note-content-field disable-tex-rendering"=
>Weaknesses:</strong> <div class=3D"note-content-value markdown-rendered"><=
p>(1) The offline graph construction cost is not sufficiently reported. SGD=
Agent relies on GPT to generate frame-level scene graphs from sampled keyfr=
ames, followed by segment/event graph construction. Since this graph index =
is central to the method, the paper should report these.</p>
<p>(2) The cross-benchmark comparison is not fully controlled. Table 1 incl=
udes results from different backbones, caption sources, and evaluation prot=
ocols, and the authors acknowledge that these should be read only as refere=
nce positions. </p>
<p>(3) The scene graph quality is not directly evaluated. Since the method =
depends on VLM-generated entities, relations, event clusters, and cross-fra=
me entity alignment. End-task QA accuracy alone is insufficient to validate=
 the reliability of the graph index.</p>
<p>(4) Many implementation details are underspecified. This limits reproduc=
ibility for an agentic system where prompts and heuristics can strongly aff=
ect results. For example, entity alignment thresholds, similarity weights, =
and entity blacklists.</p>
<p>(5) There are also some presentation issues. Section 3.2 contains a Figu=
re?? reference. Some language issues also should be polished.</p>
</div></div><div><strong class=3D"note-content-field disable-tex-rendering"=
>Review:</strong> <div class=3D"note-content-value markdown-rendered"><p>Th=
is paper proposes SGDAgent, a scene-graph-driven agent for long-form video =
understanding. The main idea is to construct a hierarchical spatio-temporal=
 scene graph index over long videos and use it to guide an agent through a =
propose-verify-answer pipeline. Instead of relying on open-ended caption re=
trieval, the method retrieves candidate events, segments, and frames from s=
tructured graph representations, then verifies evidence through targeted fr=
ame inspection. I find the problem setting important and the main idea prom=
ising. Long-form video QA often fails because the relevant evidence is spar=
se and difficult to locate, rather than because the final reasoning step is=
 intrinsically hard. A structured graph index with entity, relation, and te=
mporal anchors is a natural way to improve evidence search.</p>
<p>However, I have several concerns that prevent a stronger recommendation.=
 First, the cost and quality of the offline scene graph construction are no=
t sufficiently analyzed. Since the graph is generated using chatgpt, the me=
thod may be benefiting from substantial closed-source VLM preprocessing tha=
t is not counted in the online reasoning budget. Second, the largest gains =
mix search improvement with terminal convergence and FTT reduction, making =
it hard to isolate the actual improvement in evidence localization. Third, =
most controlled ablations are conducted on LVBench, while the broader bench=
mark comparison is not strictly matched.</p>
<p>Overall, I like the direction and believe graph-guided candidate verific=
ation is promising for long video understanding. However, the current paper=
 needs stronger cost accounting, direct graph-quality evaluation, and more =
controlled cross-benchmark ablations to fully support its claims. I therefo=
re recommend borderline.</p>
</div></div><div><strong class=3D"note-content-field disable-tex-rendering"=
>Fit:</strong> <span class=3D"note-content-value">4: Large audience</span><=
/div><div><strong class=3D"note-content-field disable-tex-rendering">Fit Ju=
stification:</strong> <div class=3D"note-content-value markdown-rendered"><=
p>The paper is well aligned with ACM Multimedia topics, including long-form=
 video understanding, multimodal reasoning, video question answering, scene=
 graphs, and agentic retrieval. The topic is relevant to a broad multimedia=
 audience.</p>
</div></div><div><strong class=3D"note-content-field disable-tex-rendering"=
>Technical Quality:</strong> <span class=3D"note-content-value">3: Good</sp=
