p>See review.</p>
</div></div><div><strong class=3D"note-content-field disable-tex-rendering"=
>Review:</strong> <div class=3D"note-content-value markdown-rendered"><h2>O=
verall Assessment</h2>
<p>This paper addresses the task of long-form video understanding by propos=
ing a Scene Graph-Driven Agent (SGDAgent) based on a hierarchical spatio-te=
mporal scene graph. The core motivation of the work, which is transforming =
traditional open-ended caption retrieval into a structured candidate verifi=
cation process grounded in a three-level "frame-clip-global event" scene gr=
aph, is conceptually sound. However, from a holistic perspective, the theor=
etical novelty of the proposed method is relatively limited, as it primaril=
y involves integrating scene graphs as retrieval indices within an existing=
 Agent framework. Furthermore, the paper suffers from notable deficiencies =
in writing and formatting, and lacks adequate discussion regarding the comp=
utational overhead and potential error propagation issues associated with i=
ts practical application. Overall, while the paper possesses some engineeri=
ng merit, a recommendation of "Weak Accept" is appropriate.</p>
<hr>
<h2>Major Weaknesses and Suggestions</h2>
<h3>1. Significant Writing and Formatting Oversights (Poor Writing and Form=
atting)</h3>
<p>The paper appears somewhat rushed in terms of fine-tuning details; it co=
ntains numerous obvious, elementary errors that severely compromise both th=
e readability and the academic rigor of the work:</p>
<p><strong>Incorrect Baseline Data Citation:</strong> In the table comparin=
g existing methods (Table 3), the authors report the results for the DVD mo=
del on the Video-MME dataset as 68.6. However, according to the original DV=
D paper, its result on Video-MME should be 67.3; the figure 68.6 actually c=
orresponds to DVD's metric on the LongVideoBench (Long) dataset. The author=
s must carefully cross-check all cited benchmark data to avoid transcriptio=
n errors that could invalidate the comparative analysis. Additionally, the =
reported results for EgoSchema do not match those in [1]; please verify thi=
s discrepancy as well.</p>
<p><strong>Spelling Errors:</strong> In Figure 1, the bottom-most label "Fr=
ame-level" is misspelled as "Fame-level." Furthermore, Figure 1 has certain=
 limitations in terms of clarity; specifically, the use of basketball as an=
 example may make it appear that the concept cannot be generalized to broad=
er, more universal methods. It might be worth considering a different examp=
le for illustrative purposes.</p>
<p><strong>Broken References:</strong> In Section 3.2 (Line 390), an unreso=
lved cross-reference appears as "as shown in Figure ??"; this reference cle=
arly intended to point to Figure 1.</p>
<h3>2. Insufficient Citation of Related Works (Missing Related Works)</h3>
<p>When discussing the compression of spatio-temporal information in long/c=
omplex videos, hierarchical graph representations, and Graph-based Agents, =
it is recommended that the following recent works be cited to broaden the s=
cope of the paper and ensure a more comprehensive literature review:</p>
<p>[1] "Building a Mind Palace: Structuring Environment-Grounded Semantic G=
raphs for Effective Long Video Analysis with LLMs", CVPR 2025.
This work also addresses the problem of long video understanding, proposing=
 to represent long video content as a hierarchically organized topological =
semantic graph (Hierarchical Graph), utilizing nodes and edges to enhance a=
n LLM's comprehension of the video. This approach bears a strong resemblanc=
e to the construction logic of the "Hierarchical Spatio-Temporal Scene Grap=
h" proposed in the current paper; comparing against this work would serve t=
o better highlight the uniqueness of the proposed toolchain design.</p>
<p>[2] "Multivariate Gaussian Representation Learning for Medical Action Ev=
aluation", arXiv:2511.10060.
This work proposes compressing fine-grained actions into adaptive 3D Gaussi=
an Tokens via a multivariate Gaussian representation, thereby filtering out=
 spatio-temporal noise while preserving action semantics. This approach sha=
res a fundamental conceptual similarity with the core idea, specifically, t=
he strategy of constructing hierarchical scene graphs to compress spatio-te=
mporal redundancy and extract key evidence from long videos. It is recommen=
ded that a discussion of this work be included around Line 159 or Line 168.=
 </p>
<p>[3] "Understanding Long Videos via LLM-Powered Entity Relation Graphs", =
arXiv:2501.15953.
This work introduces GraphVideoAgent, an agent-based framework for long vid=
eo understanding that tracks the evolution of entities within long videos b=
y explicitly modulating dynamic entity relation graphs and leveraging the r=
easoning capabilities of LLMs. This aligns closely with the core concept of=
 the current paper, which drives agents for retrieval and reasoning based o=
n Scene Graphs.</p>
<h3>3. Computational Overhead of Offline Construction</h3>
<p>The bottom-up construction of scene graphs relies on a VLM to perform gr=
aph extraction on densely sampled keyframes. When processing long videos sp=
anning hours, this approach=E2=80=94involving dense, repeated invocations=
=E2=80=94can incur substantial computational costs and API token overhead. =
We recommend that the authors include an analysis within the paper detailin=
g the specific time consumption and token costs associated with the scene g=
raph construction phase (the Offline Pipeline).</p>
<h3>4. Error Propagation and System Robustness</h3>
<p>The system's performance is highly contingent upon the quality of the un=
derlying frame-level scene graph extraction. If the underlying VLM suffers =
from hallucinations, attribute misidentification, or fails to detect critic=
al entities during the generation of frame-level scene graphs, these errors=
 will propagate upward through the aggregation process to the Global Event =
Graph.</p>
<p>How robust is the system to variations in the quality of the underlying =
scene graph generation?
[4] To what extent do the strategies outlined in the paper mitigate the pot=
entially fatal impact of such structural errors?
We recommend that the authors include a relevant robustness analysis or a b=
rief discussion to bolster the credibility of the proposed methodology.</p>
<p>[4] "Agentic Very Long Video Understanding", arXiv:2601.18157v1.</p>
