><div><strong class=3D"note-content-field disable-tex-rendering">Strengths:=
</strong> <div class=3D"note-content-value markdown-rendered"><ol>
<li><p>Clear motivation and reasonable problem formulation.
The paper identifies an important limitation of caption-centric long-video =
agents: flat text databases lack explicit entity, relation, and temporal st=
ructure. The proposed scene graph index directly addresses this issue.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Coherent framework design.
The hierarchical graph representation is intuitive and well organized. Fram=
e-level graphs capture local entity relations, segment-level graphs aggrega=
te short-term temporal interactions, and the global event graph provides hi=
gh-level event organization.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Strong matched-setting results and useful analysis.
On LVBench, SGDAgent improves end-to-end accuracy from 54.8% to 73.0% under=
 matched settings, and the trajectory case study clearly illustrates how gr=
aph-guided retrieval narrows the search space before visual verification.</=
p>
</li>
</ol>
</div></div><div><strong class=3D"note-content-field disable-tex-rendering"=
>Weaknesses:</strong> <div class=3D"note-content-value markdown-rendered"><=
ol>
<li><p>Some claims are stronger than the method supports.
The method mainly improves structured evidence retrieval and localization, =
but it does not fundamentally solve incomplete sampling coverage, since the=
 graph is still built from sparsely sampled keyframes.</p>
</li>
<li><p>The global event graph contributes more to efficiency than accuracy.
The ablation shows that adding the global event graph does not further impr=
ove accuracy, but mainly reduces LLM calls and frame-inspection calls. This=
 weakens the claim that it substantially improves high-level reasoning.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Scene graph construction is heuristic and under-analyzed.
Entity alignment, segment merging, and event construction rely on hand-desi=
gned similarity rules and thresholds. The paper does not sufficiently analy=
ze robustness to noisy entities, wrong relations, or alignment errors.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Writing and presentation require improvement.
The paper contains several presentation issues. In particular, Figure 1 and=
 Figure 2 have inconsistent font sizes and appear to be rasterized rather t=
han vector graphics, which makes some labels blurry and difficult to read. =
There is also an unresolved figure reference in Section 3.2 (=E2=80=9CFigur=
e ?? (b)=E2=80=9D), which should be corrected. In addition, the manuscript =
contains several typos and grammatical issues, such as =E2=80=9Cmulti-granu=
l,=E2=80=9D =E2=80=9Cand convert,=E2=80=9D =E2=80=9CSection 3.3 describe,=
=E2=80=9D and =E2=80=9CScene graph is ... that encode.=E2=80=9D</p>
</li>
</ol>
</div></div><div><strong class=3D"note-content-field disable-tex-rendering"=
>Review:</strong> <div class=3D"note-content-value markdown-rendered"><p>Th=
is paper proposes SGDAgent, a hierarchical spatio-temporal scene-graph-driv=
en agent for long-form video understanding. The key idea is to convert long=
 videos into structured scene graph indices at frame, segment, and global e=
vent levels, and then use an agent to perform graph-guided candidate propos=
al, visual verification, and final answering.=20
Questions:
How accurate are the generated scene graphs themselves? Is there any evalua=
tion of entity accuracy, relation accuracy, or temporal alignment quality?
How sensitive is SGDAgent to the keyframe sampling interval? Would the perf=
ormance drop significantly if the interval changes from 5 seconds to 10 sec=
onds or 2 seconds?
Since the global event graph mainly improves efficiency rather than accurac=
y, should the paper reposition it as an efficiency layer instead of a reaso=
ning layer?
How much does the full pipeline cost per video, including graph constructio=
n with gpt-4.1 and online reasoning with o4-mini or o3?</p>
</div></div><div><strong class=3D"note-content-field disable-tex-rendering"=
>Fit:</strong> <span class=3D"note-content-value">4: Large audience</span><=
/div><div><strong class=3D"note-content-field disable-tex-rendering">Fit Ju=
stification:</strong> <div class=3D"note-content-value markdown-rendered"><=
p>The paper presents a meaningful and well-motivated framework for restruct=
uring long-video search with hierarchical scene graphs. The matched-setting=
 results are strong, and the overall design is coherent. However, the claim=
s are somewhat overextended, the graph construction pipeline is heuristic, =
and the contribution of graph structure versus agent control needs clearer =
separation. These issues make the paper promising but not fully convincing.=
</p>
