-content"><div><strong class=3D"note-content-field disable-tex-rendering">S=
trengths:</strong> <div class=3D"note-content-value markdown-rendered"><ul>
<li><p>The paper tackles long-form video understanding, where flat caption-=
based retrieval struggles with sparse evidence localization, long-range tem=
poral interactions, and traceable reasoning.</p>
</li>
<li><p>The paper proposes to convert open-ended caption search into graph-a=
nchored candidate verification, using a three-level scene graph index (fram=
e/segment/event) plus an agent operating in a propose-verify-answer loop.</=
p>
</li>
<li><p>Experiment results on four benchmark datasets demonstrate substantia=
l improvements.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</div></div><div><strong class=3D"note-content-field disable-tex-rendering"=
>Weaknesses:</strong> <div class=3D"note-content-value markdown-rendered"><=
ul>
<li><p>The paper claims as a key contribution a hierarchical spatio-tempora=
l scene graph architecture and says prior work lacks unified cross-scale hi=
erarchical organization, but prior work such as HIG (CVPR 2024) already int=
roduced a hierarchical graph approach for scene graph generation in video u=
nderstanding</p>
</li>
<li><p>Several important components are described only at a high level: ent=
ity alignment uses a weighted combination of similarities with thresholding=
; event construction uses temporal gap/shared entities/relation overlap; re=
trieval uses a composite score; controller signals and routing rules are me=
ntioned but not concretely specified.</p>
</li>
<li><p>The graph-depth ablation indicates that adding the global event grap=
h does not further improve accuracy over Stage 1+2, but mainly reduces LLM =
and frame-inspection calls. That is still useful, but it weakens the impres=
sion that the full three-level hierarchy is uniformly necessary for perform=
ance.</p>
</li>
<li><p>The paper argues that the graph structure makes search more efficien=
t, but the reported efficiency evidence mainly concerns online call counts.=
</p>
</li>
<li><p>There are noticeable writing and formatting issues, including awkwar=
d grammar and an unresolved =E2=80=9CFigure ??=E2=80=9D reference.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</div></div><div><strong class=3D"note-content-field disable-tex-rendering"=
>Review:</strong> <div class=3D"note-content-value markdown-rendered"><ul>
<li><p>The paper claims a "hierarchical spatio-temporal scene graph archite=
cture" as a central contribution and explicitly states that prior methods l=
ack unified cross-scale hierarchical organization. However, HIG (CVPR 2024)=
 already proposes a Hierarchical Interlacement Graph for scene graph genera=
tion in video understanding, and describes it as a hierarchical graph struc=
ture for modeling scene changes across tasks. Because of this prior art, I =
do not think the hierarchy itself can be presented as strongly novel</p>
</li>
<li><p>After discounting the hierarchy claim somewhat, the paper=E2=80=99s =
more convincing contribution is the graph-driven propose-verify-answer pipe=
line: the graph is elevated from auxiliary representation to primary retrie=
val index, and the agent uses graph-localized proposals before visual verif=
ication. I think this is a fair and interesting contribution, but the paper=
 should position it more explicitly as the main novelty, instead of bundlin=
g it together with a representation.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Many implementation choices are too abstractly described. For exampl=
e, entity alignment depends on a weighted combination of name similarity, d=
escription similarity, and type consistency, but the weighting and threshol=
d are not given. Event construction likewise depends on temporal and semant=
ic heuristics, but the exact merging/splitting rule is unclear. The composi=
te retrieval score is specified conceptually but not operationally. The con=
troller signals and routing/gating strategies are important to the reported=
 gains, yet their instantiation remains underspecified.</p>
</li>
<li><p>The matched-setting LVBench results are compelling and, in my view, =
are the main reason the paper is above the bar. However, the paper also ack=
nowledges that the cross-benchmark comparisons are not controlled. This mak=
es the broader performance positioning less definitive than the narrative s=
ometimes suggests.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Segment-level graphs seem to provide the main accuracy benefit, whil=
e the event-level graph primarily reduces tool usage and search depth. This=
 is still a meaningful contribution, but it suggests the full hierarchy sho=
uld be presented with more nuance. In particular, the event-level graph loo=
ks more like an efficiency layer than an accuracy-critical component.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</div></div><div><strong class=3D"note-content-field disable-tex-rendering"=
>Fit:</strong> <span class=3D"note-content-value">4: Large audience</span><=
/div><div><strong class=3D"note-content-field disable-tex-rendering">Fit Ju=
stification:</strong> <div class=3D"note-content-value markdown-rendered"><=
p>The paper is an excellent fit for ACM Multimedia. It sits squarely at the=
 intersection of video understanding, multimodal reasoning, structured visu=
al representation, and agentic retrieval/search over long videos. The probl=
em setting, methodology, and benchmarks are all directly relevant to the AC=
M MM community. The paper also addresses long-form video QA with structured=
 multimodal representations, which should interest a broad portion of the m=
ultimedia retrieval and understanding audience.</p>
</div></div><div><strong class=3D"note-content-field disable-tex-rendering"=
>Technical Quality:</strong> <span class=3D"note-content-value">3: Good</sp=
an></div><div><strong class=3D"note-content-field disable-tex-rendering">Te=
chnical Presentation:</strong> <span class=3D"note-content-value">4: Good</=
span></div><div><strong class=3D"note-content-field disable-tex-rendering">=
Rating:</strong> <span class=3D"note-content-value">3: Borderline</span></d=
iv><div><strong class=3D"note-content-field disable-tex-rendering">Confiden=
ce:</strong> <span class=3D"note-content-value">3: Knowledgeable</span></di=
v><div><strong class=3D"note-content-field disable-tex-rendering">Final Rat=
ing:</strong> <span class=3D"note-content-value">4: Weak Accept</span></div=
><div><strong class=3D"note-content-field disable-tex-rendering">Justificat=
ion:</strong> <div class=3D"note-content-value markdown-rendered"><p>The au=
thors have reasonably addressed my main concerns in the rebuttal. In partic=
ular, they clarify that the contribution should be framed around graph-inde=
xed agentic evidence search rather than hierarchy alone, provide concrete i=
mplementation and cost details, and acknowledge that the event-level graph =
mainly serves as an efficiency/search-abstraction layer in the submitted ab=
lation. I would support acceptance, assuming these clarifications and promi=
sed revisions are reflected in the final version.</p>
</div></div></div></div></div><div class=3D"note  depth-odd" data-id=3D"AM5=
